 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The preprocessing step (boundary cell detection) is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of cells. The projection step is $O(B)$ where $B$ is the number of boundary cells. The ray traversal step is $O(P \cdot L)$ where $P$ is the number of viable pixels and $L$ is the average ray length within the object [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the volume data, the boundary cell flags ($O(N)$), and the Z-buffers ($O(P)$) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
 *   **Measured Performance:** The algorithm is described as "fast" and improves upon PARC by reducing projection overhead and providing more accurate boundary estimation [S1, S2, unofficial].
 *   **Scaling:** Performance scales with the number of boundary cells and viable pixels. Adaptive image sampling can help manage complexity in high-detail regions [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Fast serial rendering, accurate boundary estimation, reduced projection overhead via incremental projection, viewpoint-independent preprocessing [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Serial algorithm, may not be as efficient for very large sparse datasets as octree-based methods, incremental projection can accumulate errors [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Suitable for time-critical volume rendering where accuracy and speed are important, and the data is not extremely sparse [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** For very large, sparse datasets where octree-based methods are more efficient, or for parallel/GPU implementations where stackless traversal is preferred [S1, S2, unofficial].
